Here is an implementation of a method to reverse a doubly linked list, taking into account all the given constraints:

```
public void reverseDoublyLinkedList(Node head) {
    if (head == null) {
        return;
    }

    Node current = head;
    Node previous = null;
    Node next;

    while (current != null) {
        next = current.next;
        current.next = previous;
        current.previous = next;
        previous = current;
        current = next;
    }

    // Handling circular linked list
    if (head.previous != null) {
        head.next = head.previous;
        head.previous = previous;
    }
}
```

In this implementation, we iterate through the doubly linked list from the head to the tail, reversing the links of each node. We use three pointers - `current`, `previous`, and `next` - to keep track of the current, previous, and next nodes in the iteration.

Inside the loop, we first store the next node in the `next` variable to avoid losing the reference to the next node. Then, we update the `current` node's `next` pointer to point to the `previous` node, and the `current` node's `previous` pointer to point to the `next` node. After that, we update the `previous` node to be the `current` node and the `current` node to be the `next` node.

Once we have iterated through the entire list, we check if the list is circular by checking if the `previous` pointer of the head node is not null. If it's not null, we update the `next` pointer of the head node to point to the `previous` node, and the `previous` pointer of the head node to point to the last node in the reversed list.

