console.log(isPalindrome("Hello, world!")); // false
console.log(isPalindrome("")); // true (empty string is a palindrome)
```

This implementation uses a recursive approach to check if a substring is a palindrome. It uses two pointers, one starting from the left end of the string and the other starting from the right end of the string. The function compares the characters at the left and right positions and continues moving the pointers towards the center until they meet or cross each other. If the characters at the left and right positions are equal at each step, the function recursively checks the substring between the left and right pointers. If all characters have been checked and they are all equal, the function returns true. If at any step the characters are not equal, the function returns false.

The implementation also removes non-alphanumeric characters and converts the string to lowercase before checking for palindromes. This ensures that the method is case-insensitive and ignores any non-alphanumeric characters, as required.

