Here's a JavaScript code snippet that limits the maximum length of an input field, displays the remaining characters, and highlights the input field when the maximum length is reached:

```javascript
// Function to limit the maximum length and count remaining characters
function limitMaxLength(inputElement, maxLength) {
  // Update the remaining characters count
  function updateCount() {
    const remaining = maxLength - inputElement.value.length;
    countElement.textContent = remaining;

    // Highlight the input field when maximum length is reached
    if (remaining < 0) {
      inputElement.classList.add("max-length-reached");
    } else {
      inputElement.classList.remove("max-length-reached");
    }
  }

  // Create a count element to display the remaining characters
  const countElement = document.createElement("span");
  countElement.textContent = maxLength;
  inputElement.parentNode.appendChild(countElement);

  // Bind input event to update count
  inputElement.addEventListener("input", updateCount);

  // Call updateCount on page load
  updateCount();
}

// Usage examples
const textInput = document.getElementById("text-input");
limitMaxLength(textInput, 50);

const numberInput = document.getElementById("number-input");
limitMaxLength(numberInput, 10);

const emailInput = document.getElementById("email-input");
limitMaxLength(emailInput, 100);

const passwordInput = document.getElementById("password-input");
limitMaxLength(passwordInput, 20);
```

In this example, we create a `limitMaxLength` function that takes two arguments: the input element and the maximum length. Inside the function, we create a `countElement` (a `span` element) to display the remaining characters count, and append it to the parent of the input element.

We bind the `input` event to the input element, which calls the `updateCount` function. The `updateCount` function calculates the remaining characters by subtracting the current input value length from the maximum length. It updates the `textContent` of `countElement` with the remaining characters count.

If the remaining characters count is less than zero, indicating that the maximum length is reached, we add a CSS class (`max-length-reached`) to the input element. Otherwise, we remove the class.

Finally, we call the `limitMaxLength` function on different input elements with their respective maximum lengths.

