Here's a JavaScript function that converts a given number to an array of its prime factors:

```javascript
function getPrimeFactors(num) {
  // Input validation
  if (typeof num !== 'number' || num <= 1) {
    throw new Error('Input must be a positive number greater than 1');
  }

  const factors = [];

  // Handle 2 as a special case
  while (num % 2 === 0) {
    factors.push(2);
    num /= 2;
  }

  // Handle odd prime factors
  let factor = 3;
  while (factor * factor <= num) {
    if (num % factor === 0) {
      factors.push(factor);
      num /= factor;
    } else {
      factor += 2; // Skip even numbers
    }
  }

  // Handle remaining prime factor greater than sqrt(num)
  if (num > 1) {
    factors.push(num);
  }

  return factors;
}
```

This function first checks for input validation, ensuring that the input is a positive number greater than 1. If the input is not valid, it throws an error.

The function then initializes an empty array `factors` to store the prime factors of the given number.

The function starts by dividing the number by 2 repeatedly while it is divisible by 2. This is to handle the special case of the number being even, as 2 is the only even prime number.

After handling 2, the function starts with the factor 3 and checks if it divides the remaining number evenly. If it does, it pushes the factor to the `factors` array and divides the number by the factor. If it doesn't, it increments the factor by 2 to skip even numbers, as all other prime factors will be odd.

The loop continues until the square of the current factor is greater than the remaining number.

Finally, if the remaining number is greater than 1, it is a prime number itself, so it is added to the `factors` array.

The function returns the array `factors` containing all the prime factors of the given number.

This function efficiently handles numbers up to 10^12 by checking for prime factors only up to the square root of the given number.

