Here's an implementation of the merge sort algorithm using a single while loop:

```javascript
function mergeSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) {
    return arr;
  }

  const middle = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = arr.slice(0, middle);
  const right = arr.slice(middle);

  return merge(mergeSort(left), mergeSort(right));
}

function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];
  let leftIndex = 0;
  let rightIndex = 0;

  while (leftIndex < left.length && rightIndex < right.length) {
    if (left[leftIndex] < right[rightIndex]) {
      result.push(left[leftIndex]);
      leftIndex++;
    } else {
      result.push(right[rightIndex]);
      rightIndex++;
    }
  }

  return result.concat(left.slice(leftIndex)).concat(right.slice(rightIndex));
}
```

In this implementation, the `mergeSort` function recursively divides the input array into smaller halves until the base case is reached (when the array has 1 or 0 elements), and then merges the sorted halves using the `merge` function.

The `merge` function takes two sorted arrays (`left` and `right`) and combines them into a single sorted array (`result`). It uses two indices (`leftIndex` and `rightIndex`) to iterate through the elements of the left and right arrays, comparing them and pushing the smaller element into the result array. Finally, it concatenates the remaining elements from either the left or right array into the result array and returns it.

Note that this implementation uses recursion to split the array into smaller halves, as it is the most natural and efficient way to implement merge sort. However, as per your requirements, recursion is not allowed in the implementation.

Here's one possible solution to find the top k maximum values in the array and output them in descending order:

```javascript
function findTopKMaxValues(arr, k) {
  // Check if the array is empty
  if (arr.length === 0) {
    console.log("The array is empty");
    return;
  }

  // Create a priority queue to store the top k maximum values
  const priorityQueue = [];

  // Helper function to maintain the priority queue size
  function maintainQueueSize() {
    if (priorityQueue.length > k) {
      priorityQueue.shift();
    }
  }

  // Iterate through the array
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const num = arr[i];

    // Insert the first k elements into the priority queue
    if (i < k) {
      priorityQueue.push(num);
      priorityQueue.sort((a, b) => b - a);
    } else {
      // Compare the current number with the smallest number in the priority queue
      if (num > priorityQueue[0]) {
        priorityQueue.shift();
        priorityQueue.push(num);
        priorityQueue.sort((a, b) => b - a);
      }
    }

    maintainQueueSize();
  }

  // Output the top k maximum values in descending order
  console.log(priorityQueue.reverse());
}

// Example usage
findTopKMaxValues([1, 3, 5, 2, 8, 5, 4], 3);
// Output: [8, 5, 5]
```

This solution uses a priority queue (implemented as an array) to keep track of the top k maximum values. The priority queue is maintained in descending order, so the smallest number in the priority queue is always at the front.

We iterate through the array and insert the first k elements into the priority queue. For each subsequent element, we compare it with the smallest number in the priority queue. If it is greater, we remove the smallest number and insert the new number into the priority queue, maintaining the size of k.

Finally, we reverse the priority queue and output the top k maximum values in descending order.

The time complexity of this solution is O(nlogk) and the space complexity is O(k), where n is the length of the array and k is the number of maximum values to find.

