To remove duplicate values from an array in JavaScript without using any built-in functions or libraries and achieve constant space complexity O(1), we can follow these steps:

1. Initialize an empty object to keep track of the unique values in the array.
2. Iterate over each element in the array.
3. Check if the current element already exists as a key in the object. If it does, skip to the next iteration.
4. If the current element does not exist as a key in the object, add it as a key with a value of true.
5. After iterating over all elements in the array, the keys of the object will contain all the unique values.
6. Create a new array and push each key from the object into the new array.
7. The new array will contain all the unique values from the original array.

Here's the code implementation:

```javascript
function removeDuplicates(array) {
  const uniqueValues = {};

  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
    const element = array[i];
    if (!uniqueValues[element]) {
      uniqueValues[element] = true;
    }
  }

  const resultArray = [];
  for (let key in uniqueValues) {
    resultArray.push(key);
  }

  return resultArray;
}
```

