Here is a JavaScript program that implements the bubble sort algorithm to sort an array of numbers. The program also includes a function to reverse the sorted array:

```javascript
// Bubble Sort Algorithm
function bubbleSort(arr, n) {
  // Base case
  if (n === 1) {
    return;
  }

  // One pass of bubble sort
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
      // Swap elements
      let temp = arr[i];
      arr[i] = arr[i + 1];
      arr[i + 1] = temp;
    }
  }

  // Recursive call
  bubbleSort(arr, n - 1);
}

// Function to reverse the sorted array
function reverseArray(arr) {
  let start = 0;
  let end = arr.length - 1;

  while (start < end) {
    // Swap elements
    let temp = arr[start];
    arr[start] = arr[end];
    arr[end] = temp;

    start++;
    end--;
  }
}

// Test array
let numbers = [5, 3, 8, 2, 1, 9, 5, 6, 3, 7];

// Sort the array
bubbleSort(numbers, numbers.length);

// Reverse the sorted array
reverseArray(numbers);

// Print the sorted array in reverse order
console.log(numbers);
```

This program uses a recursive function `bubbleSort` to sort the array using the bubble sort algorithm. It also includes a `reverseArray` function to reverse the sorted array. Finally, it prints the sorted array in reverse order using `console.log`.

