Here's a JavaScript program that classifies a given array of strings into four groups - integers, floating-point numbers, hexadecimal numbers, and non-parsable strings. It also sorts each group in ascending order, ignoring leading and trailing whitespaces:

```javascript
function classifyStrings(arr) {
  const integers = [];
  const floats = [];
  const hexadecimals = [];
  const nonParsables = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    let str = arr[i].trim();

    // Check if string can be parsed into an integer
    if (/^-?\d+$/.test(str)) {
      integers.push(parseInt(str, 10));
      continue;
    }

    // Check if string can be parsed into a floating-point number
    if (/^-?\d+(\.\d+)?$/.test(str)) {
      floats.push(parseFloat(str));
      continue;
    }

    // Check if string can be parsed into a hexadecimal number
    if (/^0x[0-9a-f]+$/i.test(str)) {
      hexadecimals.push(parseInt(str, 16));
      continue;
    }

    nonParsables.push(str);
  }

  sortCustom(integers);
  sortCustom(floats);
  sortCustom(hexadecimals);

  return {
    integers: integers,
    floats: floats,
    hexadecimals: hexadecimals,
    nonParsables: nonParsables
  };
}

// Custom sorting algorithm for integers
function sortCustom(arr) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < arr.length - i - 1; j++) {
      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
        let temp = arr[j];
        arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
        arr[j + 1] = temp;
      }
    }
  }
}

// Example usage
const arr = ['10', '3.14', '0x1A', '20', 'abc', '  5  ', '8.6', '0x20', '12.5', '0xFF'];
const result = classifyStrings(arr);

console.log('Integers:', result.integers);
console.log('Floats:', result.floats);
console.log('Hexadecimals:', result.hexadecimals);
console.log('Non-parsables:', result.nonParsables);
```

This program first defines four empty arrays - `integers`, `floats`, `hexadecimals`, and `nonParsables`. Then, it loops through each element of the given array and checks if it can be parsed into an integer, floating-point number, or hexadecimal number using regular expressions.

If a string can be parsed into an integer, it is parsed using `parseInt()` with base 10 and added to the `integers` array. If it can be parsed into a floating-point number, it is parsed using `parseFloat()` and added to the `floats` array. If it can be parsed into a hexadecimal number, it is parsed using `parseInt()` with base 16 and added to the `hexadecimals` array. If it cannot be parsed into any of the above formats, it is added to the `nonParsables` array.

After classifying the strings, the program calls the `sortCustom()` function to sort the `integers`, `floats`, and `hexadecimals` arrays using a custom sorting algorithm.

Finally, the program returns an object containing the four groups - `integers`, `floats`, `hexadecimals`, and `nonParsables`. This object is then logged to the console for demonstration purposes.

Note: The custom sorting algorithm used in this program is a basic implementation of bubble sort. It can be further optimized for large arrays.

