```javascript
function countCommonCharacters(str1, str2) {
  // Remove special characters and whitespace from both strings
  str1 = removeSpecialCharacters(str1);
  str2 = removeSpecialCharacters(str2);

  // Convert both strings to lowercase for case-insensitive comparison
  str1 = str1.toLowerCase();
  str2 = str2.toLowerCase();

  // Initialize a count variable to keep track of the common characters count
  let count = 0;

  // Create an array of size 128 to store the frequency of characters
  const frequency = Array(128).fill(0);

  // Loop through the first string and increment the corresponding character frequency
  for (let i = 0; i < str1.length; i++) {
    const charCode = str1.charCodeAt(i);
    frequency[charCode]++;
  }

  // Loop through the second string and decrement the corresponding character frequency
  // for every common character
  for (let i = 0; i < str2.length; i++) {
    const charCode = str2.charCodeAt(i);
    if (frequency[charCode] > 0) {
      count++;
      frequency[charCode]--;
    }
  }

  // Return the count of common characters
  return count;
}

// Helper function to remove special characters and whitespace from a string
function removeSpecialCharacters(str) {
  let result = "";
  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
    const charCode = str.charCodeAt(i);
    if (charCode >= 48 && charCode <= 57) {
      // Character is a digit (0-9)
      result += str[i];
    } else if (charCode >= 65 && charCode <= 90) {
      // Character is an uppercase letter (A-Z)
      result += str[i];
    } else if (charCode >= 97 && charCode <= 122) {
      // Character is a lowercase letter (a-z)
      result += str[i];
    }
  }
  return result;
}

// Example usage
console.log(countCommonCharacters("Hello123", "hello456")); // Output: 5
```

In this program, we start by removing any special characters and whitespace from both strings using the `removeSpecialCharacters` helper function. Then, we convert both strings to lowercase for case-insensitive comparison.

Next, we initialize a count variable to keep track of the common characters count. We also create an array called `frequency` of size 128 to store the frequency of characters. Each index of the array represents a character's ASCII code.

We loop through the first string and increment the corresponding character frequency in the `frequency` array. Then, we loop through the second string and decrement the corresponding character frequency for every common character. If the frequency of a character is greater than 0, it means it's a common character, and we increment the count variable and decrement the frequency.

Finally, we return the count of common characters.

Note that this program assumes that both input strings only contain characters from the ASCII range 0-127. If the input strings contain characters outside this range, you may need to modify the program accordingly.

