Then, it determines whether to rotate the array to the right or left based on the sign of `positions`. It calculates the actual amount to rotate by taking the absolute value of `positions` modulo the length of the array. If the result is 0, it means the array doesn't need to be rotated and the original array is returned.

For rotating the array to the right, it uses two nested loops. The outer loop iterates `rotateAmount` times, each time rotating the array by moving each element one position to the right. The inner loop starts from the last element and moves it to the front of the array, and then moves all other elements one position to the right.

For rotating the array to the left, it uses a similar approach with two nested loops. The outer loop iterates `rotateAmount` times, each time rotating the array by moving each element one position to the left. The inner loop starts from the first element and moves it to the end of the array, and then moves all other elements one position to the left.

