To solve this problem, we can use the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to generate all prime numbers up to a given number `n`. We can then calculate the sum of all these prime numbers.

Here is the JavaScript code for the algorithm:

```javascript
function sumOfPrimes(n) {
  // Create an array to store whether a number is prime or not
  const isPrime = new Array(n + 1).fill(true);

  // 0 and 1 are not prime
  isPrime[0] = isPrime[1] = false;

  // Find all prime numbers up to n using the Sieve of Eratosthenes
  for (let i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
    if (isPrime[i]) {
      for (let j = i * i; j <= n; j += i) {
        isPrime[j] = false;
      }
    }
  }

  let sum = 0;

  // Calculate the sum of all prime numbers up to n
  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    if (isPrime[i]) {
      sum += i;
    }
  }

  return sum;
}

// Test the algorithm
console.log(sumOfPrimes(10)); // Output: 17
console.log(sumOfPrimes(100)); // Output: 24133
```

The algorithm first initializes an array `isPrime` of size `n+1` and sets all elements to `true`. Then, it marks `0` and `1` as non-prime since they are not prime numbers.

Next, it starts from the number `2` and iterates up to the square root of `n`. For each number `i`, if `isPrime[i]` is `true`, it means `i` is prime. So, it iterates from `i*i` up to `n`, incrementing by `i`, and marks all these numbers as non-prime by setting `isPrime[j]` to `false`.

Finally, it calculates the sum of all prime numbers by iterating from `2` up to `n` and adding each prime number `i` to the sum if `isPrime[i]` is `true`.

The time complexity of this algorithm is `O(n log log n)` because the outer loop runs approximately `n/log(n)` times, and the inner loop runs approximately `n/2 + n/3 + n/5 + n/7 + ... = n * (1/2 + 1/3 + 1/5 + 1/7 + ...)`, which is bounded by `n * log(log(n))` using the properties of the harmonic series.

The space complexity is `O(n)` because we use an array of size `n+1` to store whether a number is prime or not.

