console.log(sums);
```

The program starts by initializing the sum variables: `positiveSum`, `negativeSum`, and `zeroSum`, to 0.

It then creates a `Set` called `uniquePositiveNumbers` to store unique positive numbers encountered in the array.

Next, it loops through each number in the array and checks if it is positive, negative, or zero. For positive numbers, it adds them to the `uniquePositiveNumbers` set. For negative numbers, it squares them and adds the result to the `negativeSum`. For zero numbers, it squares them and adds the result to the `zeroSum`.

After the loop, it iterates over the `uniquePositiveNumbers` set and squares each positive number, adding the result to the `positiveSum`.

Finally, it returns an object with the three sum variables: `positiveSum`, `negativeSum`, and `zeroSum`.

The time complexity of this program is O(n), where n is the length of the input array. This is because it only loops through the array once. The space complexity is O(k), where k is the number of unique positive numbers encountered in the array.

