2. Initialize an empty `Set` called `seen` to keep track of the numbers we have seen so far.
3. Iterate through each number in the input array using a for loop.
4. Calculate the complement of the current number by subtracting it from the target. This represents the number that, when added to the current number, will give the target.
5. Check if the complement is present in the `seen` set. If it is, it means we have already seen a number that, when added to the current number, gives the target. In that case, we add the complement to the `sum`.
6. Add the current number to the `seen` set.
7. Repeat steps 4-6 for all numbers in the array.
8. Return the `sum`.

The time complexity of this function is O(n) because we iterate through each number in the array once. The `Set` operations (such as `has()` and `add()`) have an average time complexity of O(1). Thus, the overall time complexity is O(n).

