function areArraysEqual(arr1, arr2) {
  // Create two pointers to track the indices of the arrays
  let i = 0;
  let j = 0;

  // Create two stacks to track the nested arrays
  const stack1 = [arr1];
  const stack2 = [arr2];

  while (stack1.length > 0 && stack2.length > 0) {
    // Get the current arrays from the stacks
    const currArr1 = stack1[stack1.length - 1];
    const currArr2 = stack2[stack2.length - 1];

    // Check if the indices are out of bounds
    if (i >= currArr1.length || j >= currArr2.length) {
      stack1.pop();
      stack2.pop();
      i = i === currArr1.length ? i + 1 : i;
      j = j === currArr2.length ? j + 1 : j;
      continue;
    }

    // Get the current elements from the arrays
    const elem1 = currArr1[i];
    const elem2 = currArr2[j];

    // Check if the current elements are arrays
    if (Array.isArray(elem1) && Array.isArray(elem2)) {
      // Push the nested arrays onto the stacks
      stack1.push(elem1);
      stack2.push(elem2);
      // Reset the indices
      i = 0;
      j = 0;
    } else {
      // Check if the current elements are not equal
      if (elem1 !== elem2) {
        return false;
      }
      // Increment the indices
      i++;
      j++;
    }
  }

  // Check if the stacks are empty
  return stack1.length === 0 && stack2.length === 0;
}
```

This function uses two pointers `i` and `j` to track the indices of the arrays `arr1` and `arr2`, respectively. It also uses two stacks `stack1` and `stack2` to track the nested arrays within `arr1` and `arr2`, respectively.

The function then enters a loop that continues until both stacks are empty. In each iteration of the loop, the function gets the current arrays from the top of the stacks and checks if the indices are out of bounds. If the indices are out of bounds, the function pops the arrays from the stacks and increments the indices accordingly.

If the indices are not out of bounds, the function gets the current elements from the arrays and checks if they are arrays. If both elements are arrays, the function pushes them onto the stacks and resets the indices to 0. If the current elements are not arrays, the function checks if they are equal. If the elements are not equal, the function returns `false`.

Finally, after the loop, the function checks if both stacks are empty. If both stacks are empty, it means that all the elements in the arrays have been compared and found to be equal, so the function returns `true`. Otherwise, if either stack is not empty, it means that there are additional elements in one of the arrays, so the function returns `false`.

