Here's a JavaScript program that uses recursion to find the minimum of three numbers stored in an array:

```javascript
function findMinimum(arr, index = 0) {
  // Base case: if index reaches the last element, return that element
  if (index === arr.length - 1) {
    return arr[index];
  }

  // Recursive case: compare the current element with the minimum of the rest of the array
  const current = arr[index];
  const minimumOfRest = findMinimum(arr, index + 1);

  // Return the smaller value between current and minimumOfRest
  return (current < minimumOfRest) ? current : minimumOfRest;
}

// Example usage
const numbers = [7, 3, 9];
const minimum = findMinimum(numbers);
console.log(minimum); // Output: 3
```

In this program, the `findMinimum` function takes two arguments: the array of numbers (`arr`) and an optional index (`index`) that keeps track of the current position in the array. The index is initialized to 0 by default.

The function first checks if the current index is pointing to the last element of the array. If so, it returns that element, as it is the minimum value.

If the current index is not pointing to the last element, the function recursively calls itself with the next index (`index + 1`). It then compares the current element (`arr[index]`) with the minimum value returned from the recursive call (`minimumOfRest`).

Finally, the function returns the smaller value between the current element and the minimum of the rest of the array.

To use the function, you can pass an array of three numbers (`numbers`) and assign the result of `findMinimum(numbers)` to a variable (`minimum`). Then, you can print the value of `minimum` to the console. In the example above, the output will be `3`.

