The first step is to iterate through the input string to check for a match with the pattern. We will start at index 0 and continue until the length of the string minus the length of the pattern.

Inside the loop, we will compare each character of the pattern with the corresponding character in the string. We will handle the different wildcard characters as follows:

- For the '*' wildcard, we will recursively check if the substring starting from the current index of the string matches the substring starting from the current index of the pattern.
- For the '?' wildcard, we will consider it a match for any character in the string.
- For the '+' wildcard, we will consider it a match only if the corresponding character in the string is a digit.

If at any point the characters do not match, we will break out of the loop and continue to the next index in the string.

If we reach the end of the loop without breaking, it means we have found a match. We will return the current index as the starting index of the pattern in the string.

Here is the implementation in Python:

def find_pattern(string, pattern):
    str_len = len(string)
    pat_len = len(pattern)

    for i in range(str_len - pat_len + 1):
        match = True

        for j in range(pat_len):
            if pattern[j] == '*':
                return find_pattern(string[i:], pattern[j+1:])
            elif pattern[j] == '?':
                continue
            elif pattern[j] == '+':
                if not string[i+j].isdigit():
                    match = False
                    break
            elif string[i+j] != pattern[j]:
                match = False
                break

        if match:
            return i

    return -1

