To construct an array of unique prime integers from 1 to 10000 in ascending order with the desired time and space complexity, we can use the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm. Here's the implementation in JavaScript:

```javascript
function findPrimes(n) {
  // Create an array of boolean values, indicating whether a number is prime or not
  const isPrime = new Array(n + 1).fill(true);
  isPrime[0] = false;
  isPrime[1] = false;

  // Apply the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm
  for (let i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
    if (isPrime[i]) {
      for (let j = i * i; j <= n; j += i) {
        isPrime[j] = false;
      }
    }
  }

  // Collect the prime numbers in an array
  const primes = [];
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
    if (isPrime[i]) {
      primes.push(i);
    }
  }

  return primes;
}

// Test the function
const primesArray = findPrimes(10000);
console.log(primesArray);
```

The `findPrimes` function uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to determine the prime numbers up to a given number `n`. It starts by assuming all numbers are prime (`isPrime` array) and then iteratively marks the multiples of each prime number as non-prime. Finally, it collects the prime numbers in the `primes` array and returns it.

The time complexity of this implementation is O(n log log n) because for each number up to n, we only need to check its multiples once. The space complexity is O(n) because we need an array of size n to store the boolean values indicating whether a number is prime or not.

