Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that focuses on creating objects that contain both data and methods to manipulate that data. It is based on the concept of classes, which are blueprints or templates for creating objects.

In JavaScript, OOP can be implemented using classes and objects. Starting from ECMAScript 6 (ES6), JavaScript introduced the class syntax to make it easier to define classes and create objects. Here is an example of a class definition in JavaScript:

```javascript
class Person {
  constructor(name, age) {
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
  }

  sayHello() {
    console.log(`Hello, my name is ${this.name}`);
  }
}
```

In this example, we define a `Person` class with a constructor function that sets the `name` and `age` properties of each `Person` object. The `sayHello` method logs a greeting message with the person's name.

We can then create instances of the `Person` class using the `new` keyword:

```javascript
const person1 = new Person('Alice', 25);
const person2 = new Person('Bob', 30);

person1.sayHello(); // Output: Hello, my name is Alice
person2.sayHello(); // Output: Hello, my name is Bob
```

Inheritance is another important concept in OOP. It allows classes to inherit properties and methods from other classes, forming a hierarchical relationship. In JavaScript, we can achieve inheritance using the `extends` keyword. Here's an example:

```javascript
class Student extends Person {
  constructor(name, age, grade) {
    super(name, age);
    this.grade = grade;
  }

  study() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is studying.`);
  }
}
```

In this example, the `Student` class extends the `Person` class using `extends`. The `super` keyword is used to call the parent class constructor. The `Student` class also has its own `study` method.

```javascript
const student1 = new Student('Carol', 18, 'A');
student1.sayHello(); // Output: Hello, my name is Carol
student1.study(); // Output: Carol is studying.
```

Polymorphism is the ability of objects to take on different forms based on their class hierarchy. In JavaScript, polymorphism can be achieved by defining methods with the same name in different classes. Here's an example:

```javascript
class Shape {
  draw() {
    console.log('Drawing a shape.');
  }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
  draw() {
    console.log('Drawing a circle.');
  }
}

class Rectangle extends Shape {
  draw() {
    console.log('Drawing a rectangle.');
  }
