3. Learning curve: Understanding and implementing OOP concepts in JavaScript may require additional time and effort to learn and grasp.

A common mistake when implementing inheritance in JavaScript is not correctly setting the prototype chain. Consider the following incorrect example:

```javascript
function Animal(name) {
  this.name = name;
}

function Dog(name, breed) {
  this.breed = breed;
}

Dog.prototype = Animal.prototype;

const dog = new Dog('Buddy', 'Labrador');
console.log(dog.name); // Output: undefined
```

In this example, the prototype of `Dog` is set to `Animal` incorrectly. As a result, when creating a new `Dog` object, the `name` property is undefined. To fix this, we should set the prototype of `Dog` to an instance of `Animal`, not the `Animal.prototype` itself:

```javascript
function Animal(name) {
  this.name = name;
}

function Dog(name, breed) {
  this.breed = breed;
}

Dog.prototype = Object.create(Animal.prototype);
Dog.prototype.constructor = Dog;

const dog = new Dog('Buddy', 'Labrador');
console.log(dog.name); // Output: Buddy
```

In this corrected example, we use `Object.create()` to set the prototype of `Dog` to an instance of `Animal`, and we also assign the `constructor` property to `Dog` explicitly.

