Here is a JavaScript code that efficiently prints all possible combinations of substrings of a given string:

```javascript
function getAllSubstrings(input) {
  const substrings = new Set();

  for (let i = 0; i < input.length; i++) {
    for (let j = i + 1; j <= input.length; j++) {
      substrings.add(input.slice(i, j));
    }
  }

  return Array.from(substrings).sort();
}

const input = "abcd";
const output = getAllSubstrings(input);
console.log(output);
```

This code uses a nested loop to generate all possible substrings of the input string. It starts by iterating over each character of the input string, and for each character, it iterates over the remaining characters to generate substrings of different lengths. Each generated substring is added to a set to eliminate duplicates.

Finally, the set is converted to an array and sorted lexicographically using the `sort()` method. The sorted array is then printed to the console.

The time complexity of this code is O(n^2) where n is the length of the input string. Since the code uses a set to eliminate duplicates, it ensures that the output does not contain any duplicate substrings.

