To solve this problem, we can use a recursive function that takes an array as input and returns an array containing only the even numbers.

The base case of the recursive function is when the input array is empty. In this case, we return an empty array.

For the recursive case, we check if the first element of the array is even. If it is, we add it to the result array and make a recursive call to the function with the rest of the array as input. If the first element is not even, we simply make a recursive call to the function with the rest of the array as input.

Here is the implementation of the `filterEvenNumbers` function:

```javascript
function filterEvenNumbers(arr) {
  // Base case: empty array
  if (arr.length === 0) {
    return [];
  }

  // Recursive case
  const firstElement = arr[0];
  const restOfArray = arr.slice(1);

  if (isEven(firstElement)) {
    return [firstElement, ...filterEvenNumbers(restOfArray)];
  } else {
    return filterEvenNumbers(restOfArray);
  }
}

function isEven(num) {
  // Check if the number is an integer and divisible by 2
  return Number.isInteger(num) && num % 2 === 0;
}
```

