  var elementTop = element.offset().top;
  var elementBottom = elementTop + element.outerHeight();
  var viewportTop = $(window).scrollTop();
  var viewportBottom = viewportTop + viewportHeight;

  return elementBottom > viewportTop && elementTop < viewportBottom;
}

function isScrolledToPosition(element) {
  var desiredPosition = element.offset().top + element.outerHeight() + 100;
  var viewportTop = $(window).scrollTop();

  return viewportTop >= desiredPosition;
}
```

In this example, the `$(document).ready()` function ensures that the code is executed only after the document has been fully loaded.

The `$(window).on('scroll', function() { ... })` attaches a scroll event listener to the window object. Whenever the user scrolls, the function inside the event listener is triggered.

Inside the scroll event function, the code first selects the element with the "animated" class and the "start-animation" data attribute using the `$('.animated[data-start-animation]')` selector.

The `isElementInView()` function checks if the element is in the viewport by comparing its top and bottom positions with the viewport's top and bottom positions. It uses jQuery's `offset()`, `outerHeight()`, and `scrollTop()` methods to calculate these positions.

The `isScrolledToPosition()` function calculates the desired position for the animation to start. It adds the element's top position, outer height, and an additional 100 pixels to get the desired position. It then compares this position with the current viewport top position.

If both `isElementInView()` and `isScrolledToPosition()` return `true`, it means that the element is in the viewport and the user has scrolled to the desired position. In this case, the animation is initiated using the `animate()` method. It sets the background color to transition from red to green over 5 seconds (specified as 5000 milliseconds). You can modify the background color and duration as needed.

