```java
public class Vector3D {
    private double x;
    private double y;
    private double z;

    public Vector3D(double x, double y, double z) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
        this.z = z;
    }

    public Vector3D add(Vector3D other) {
        return new Vector3D(this.x + other.x, this.y + other.y, this.z + other.z);
    }

    public Vector3D subtract(Vector3D other) {
        return new Vector3D(this.x - other.x, this.y - other.y, this.z - other.z);
    }

    public Vector3D scalarMultiply(double scalar) {
        return new Vector3D(this.x * scalar, this.y * scalar, this.z * scalar);
    }

    public double dotProduct(Vector3D other) {
        return this.x * other.x + this.y * other.y + this.z * other.z;
    }

    public Vector3D crossProduct(Vector3D other) {
        double newX = this.y * other.z - this.z * other.y;
        double newY = this.z * other.x - this.x * other.z;
        double newZ = this.x * other.y - this.y * other.x;
        return new Vector3D(newX, newY, newZ);
    }

    public Vector3D normalize() {
        double magnitude = this.magnitude();
        return new Vector3D(this.x / magnitude, this.y / magnitude, this.z / magnitude);
    }

    public double angleWith(Vector3D other) {
        double dotProduct = this.dotProduct(other);
        double magnitudeProduct = this.magnitude() * other.magnitude();
        double cosTheta = dotProduct / magnitudeProduct;
        return Math.toDegrees(Math.acos(cosTheta));
    }

    public double magnitude() {
        return Math.sqrt(this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y + this.z * this.z);
    }

    public Vector3D projectOnto(Vector3D other) {
        double dotProduct = this.dotProduct(other);
        double magnitudeSquared = other.magnitude() * other.magnitude();
        double scalar = dotProduct / magnitudeSquared;
        return other.scalarMultiply(scalar);
    }
}
```

This implementation stores the vector components (`x`, `y`, `z`) as private fields of the class. The constructor initializes these fields when creating a new instance of the `Vector3D` class.

The `add` method adds the components of another vector to the current vector and returns a new `Vector3D` object representing the sum.

The `subtract` method subtracts the components of another vector from the current vector and returns a new `Vector3D` object representing the difference.

The `scalarMultiply` method multiplies each component of the vector by a scalar value and returns a new `Vector3D` object representing the scaled vector.

The `dotProduct` method calculates the dot product of the current vector with another vector.

The `crossProduct` method calculates the cross product of the current vector with another vector and returns a new `Vector3D` object representing the result.

The `normalize` method calculates the normalized vector by dividing each component of the vector by its magnitude and returns a new `Vector3D` object representing the normalized vector.

The `angleWith` method calculates the angle between the current vector and another vector in degrees using the dot product and magnitude.

The `magnitude` method calculates the magnitude of the vector using the Pythagorean theorem.

The `projectOnto` method calculates the projection of the current vector onto another vector using the dot product and magnitude.

All methods have a time complexity of O(1) since they perform a fixed number of arithmetic operations. The space complexity is also O(1) as no additional space is used to store the vector components or intermediate results. Method chaining is supported by returning a new `Vector3D` object in each method.

