Here's a recursive function in JavaScript that performs a bubble sort of an array:

```javascript
function bubbleSortRecursive(arr) {
  let swapped = false;

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
    if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
      [arr[i], arr[i + 1]] = [arr[i + 1], arr[i]];
      swapped = true;
    }
  }

  if (swapped) {
    return bubbleSortRecursive(arr);
  } else {
    return arr;
  }
}
```

This function follows the steps outlined in the requirements:

1. Initializes a variable "swapped" to keep track of whether any swaps have been made in a pass.
2. Uses a while loop that continues as long as the "swapped" variable is true (implemented using recursion instead).
3. Sets the "swapped" variable to false at the beginning of each pass.
4. Iterates through the array from the first element to the second-to-last element.
5. Compares each pair of adjacent elements.
6. If the current element is greater than the next element, swaps them and sets the "swapped" variable to true.
7. After each pass, the largest element in the array will have "bubbled" up to the end of the array.
8. Repeats the above steps until no more swaps are made in a pass, indicating that the array is fully sorted.

The function has a time complexity of O(n^2), where n is the length of the array, as each pass through the array requires comparing each pair of adjacent elements.

