To calculate the sum of all prime numbers between 3 and 17, inclusive, you can use the following code:

```javascript
function isPrime(n) {
  if (n === 2) return true;
  if (n < 2 || n % 2 === 0) return false;

  for (let i = 3; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i += 2) {
    if (n % i === 0) {
      return false;
    }
  }

  return true;
}

function sumPrimes(lower, upper) {
  if (lower > upper) return 0;

  if (isPrime(lower)) {
    return lower + sumPrimes(lower + 1, upper);
  } else {
    return sumPrimes(lower + 1, upper);
  }
}

console.log(sumPrimes(3, 17)); // Output: 58
```

In this code, we have two functions: `isPrime` and `sumPrimes`.

The `isPrime` function takes a number `n` as input and checks if it is a prime number. It uses a simple algorithm to iterate through all odd numbers up to the square root of `n` to check for divisibility. If `n` is divisible by any of these numbers, it is not a prime number and the function returns `false`. Otherwise, it returns `true`.

The `sumPrimes` function takes two parameters: `lower` and `upper`, which represent the lower and upper limits of the range, respectively. It uses recursion to iterate through each number in the range and adds it to the sum if it is a prime number. If `lower` becomes greater than `upper`, it returns `0` to end the recursion.

In the main code, we call `sumPrimes` with the range from 3 to 17 and log the result to the console, which should output `58` as the sum of all prime numbers between 3 and 17, inclusive.

