Here's an example of a function in JavaScript that checks if a given string contains at least three consecutive vowels followed by two or more consecutive consonants:

```javascript
function containsThreeVowels(str) {
  const vowels = ['a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'];
  const consonants = ['b', 'c', 'd', 'f', 'g', 'h', 'j', 'k', 'l', 'm', 'n', 'p', 'q', 'r', 's', 't', 'v', 'w', 'x', 'y', 'z'];

  let consecutiveVowels = 0;
  let consecutiveConsonants = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
    const char = str[i].toLowerCase();

    if (vowels.includes(char)) {
      consecutiveVowels++;
      consecutiveConsonants = 0;

      if (consecutiveVowels >= 3 && consecutiveConsonants >= 2) {
        return true;
      }
    } else if (consonants.includes(char)) {
      consecutiveConsonants++;
      consecutiveVowels = 0;
    } else {
      consecutiveVowels = 0;
      consecutiveConsonants = 0;
    }
  }

  return false;
}

// Example usage:
console.log(containsThreeVowels('hello')); // false
console.log(containsThreeVowels('aeioubcdf')); // true
console.log(containsThreeVowels('consecutive')); // true
console.log(containsThreeVowels('abcde')); // false
```

This function uses two arrays: one for vowels and another for consonants. It iterates through the input string character by character, keeping track of the number of consecutive vowels and consonants. If at any point it finds at least three consecutive vowels followed by two or more consecutive consonants, it returns `true`. Otherwise, it returns `false`.

