One way to implement this Data Structure is by using a min heap. A min heap is a binary tree where the value of each node is smaller than or equal to the values of its children. In this case, we will store tasks as objects with two properties: "task" and "priority".

Here's an example implementation:

```javascript
class TaskQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.tasks = [];
  }

  addTask(task, priority) {
    this.tasks.push({ task, priority });
    this.bubbleUp(this.tasks.length - 1);
  }

  completeTask() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return null;
    }

    const highestPriorityTask = this.tasks[0];
    const lastTask = this.tasks.pop();

    if (this.tasks.length > 0) {
      this.tasks[0] = lastTask;
      this.bubbleDown(0);
    }

    return highestPriorityTask.task;
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.tasks.length === 0;
  }

  getPriority() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return null;
    }

    return this.tasks[0].priority;
  }

  getNumTasks() {
    return this.tasks.length;
  }

  bubbleUp(index) {
    const task = this.tasks[index];

    while (index > 0) {
      const parentIndex = Math.floor((index - 1) / 2);
      const parentTask = this.tasks[parentIndex];

      if (task.priority >= parentTask.priority) {
        break;
      }

      this.tasks[parentIndex] = task;
      this.tasks[index] = parentTask;
      index = parentIndex;
    }
  }

  bubbleDown(index) {
    const task = this.tasks[index];
    const length = this.tasks.length;

    while (true) {
      let leftChildIndex = 2 * index + 1;
      let rightChildIndex = 2 * index + 2;
      let leftChildTask, rightChildTask;
      let swap = null;

      if (leftChildIndex < length) {
        leftChildTask = this.tasks[leftChildIndex];
        if (leftChildTask.priority < task.priority) {
          swap = leftChildIndex;
        }
      }

      if (rightChildIndex < length) {
        rightChildTask = this.tasks[rightChildIndex];
        if (
          (swap === null && rightChildTask.priority < task.priority) ||
          (swap !== null && rightChildTask.priority < leftChildTask.priority)
        ) {
          swap = rightChildIndex;
        }
      }

      if (swap === null) {
        break;
      }

      this.tasks[index] = this.tasks[swap];
      this.tasks[swap] = task;
      index = swap;
    }
  }
}
```

In this implementation, the `addTask()` method adds a task to the array and then calls the `bubbleUp()` method to ensure that the task is in the correct position in the min heap.

The `completeTask()` method removes the task with the highest priority (at the root of the min heap), replaces it with the last task in the array, and then calls the `bubbleDown()` method to restore the min heap property.

The `isEmpty()` method simply checks if the tasks array is empty.

The `getPriority()` method returns the priority of the task at the root of the min heap.

The `getNumTasks()` method returns the number of tasks in the array.

Both `bubbleUp()` and `bubbleDown()` methods are helper methods to maintain the min heap property when adding or removing tasks. They have time complexity O(log n) since the height of a complete binary tree (which is what a min heap is) is log n.

