To remove a specific key from a JavaScript object while preserving the structure of the remaining data, we can recursively iterate through the object and its nested structures. Here's how we can implement the `removeKey` function:

```javascript
function removeKey(obj, key) {
  // Check if obj is an array
  if (Array.isArray(obj)) {
    // Create a new array to store the modified values
    const newArray = [];
    // Iterate through each element in the array
    for (let i = 0; i < obj.length; i++) {
      // Recursively call removeKey for each element in the array
      const modifiedElement = removeKey(obj[i], key);
      // Push the modified element into the new array
      newArray.push(modifiedElement);
    }
    // Return the modified array
    return newArray;
  }

  // Check if obj is an object
  if (typeof obj === 'object' && obj !== null) {
    // Create a new object to store the modified properties
    const newObject = {};
    // Iterate through each property in the object
    for (const prop in obj) {
      // Check if the current property is the key to be removed
      if (prop === key) {
        // Skip the current property
        continue;
      }
      // Recursively call removeKey for the current property value
      const modifiedValue = removeKey(obj[prop], key);
      // Add the modified property to the new object
      newObject[prop] = modifiedValue;
    }
    // Return the modified object
    return newObject;
  }

  // Return the value as is if it is neither an array nor an object
  return obj;
}
```

In the `removeKey` function, we first check if `obj` is an array. If it is, we create a new array and iterate through each element. For each element, we recursively call `removeKey` to remove the specified key from that element and push the modified element into the new array.

Next, we check if `obj` is an object. If it is, we create a new object and iterate through each property. For each property, we check if it is the key to be removed. If it is, we skip the current property. Otherwise, we recursively call `removeKey` to remove the specified key from the property value and add the modified property to the new object.

Finally, if `obj` is neither an array nor an object, we simply return the value as is.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the total number of keys in the object, as we need to iterate through each key in the object and its nested structures.

