Here's a correct implementation of the program that meets the given requirements:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>

int countSubarrayFrequency(const std::vector<int>& arr, const std::vector<int>& subarr) {
    int count = 0;
    int n = arr.size();
    int m = subarr.size();

    std::unordered_map<int, int> prefixSumFreq;
    int prefixSum = 0;

    prefixSumFreq[0] = 1;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        prefixSum += arr[i];

        if (prefixSumFreq.find(prefixSum - m) != prefixSumFreq.end()) {
            count += prefixSumFreq[prefixSum - m];
        }

        prefixSumFreq[prefixSum]++;
    }

    return count;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6};
    std::vector<int> subarr = {2, 3};

    int frequency = countSubarrayFrequency(arr, subarr);

    if (frequency > 0) {
        std::cout << "Frequency of subarray: " << frequency << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Subarray not found." << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program uses the concept of prefix sums to calculate the frequency of a subarray in an array. It maintains a hash table to store the frequency of each prefix sum encountered while iterating through the array. For each prefix sum, it checks if there is a prefix sum that differs by the length of the subarray. If such a prefix sum exists, it adds its frequency to the count. Finally, it returns the count as the frequency of the subarray.

The time complexity of this program is O(n) because it iterates through the array only once. The use of a hash table allows constant time lookup and insertion of prefix sums, resulting in an efficient solution.

