Here is a recursive JavaScript function that checks whether a string is a palindrome, ignoring non-alphanumeric characters and case sensitivity:

```javascript
function isPalindrome(str) {
  // Helper function to check if a character is alphanumeric
  function isAlphanumeric(char) {
    var code = char.charCodeAt(0);
    return (code >= 48 && code <= 57) || // Numeric (0-9)
           (code >= 65 && code <= 90) || // Uppercase alphabets (A-Z)
           (code >= 97 && code <= 122); // Lowercase alphabets (a-z)
  }

  // Recursive function to check if the remaining characters are palindrome
  function isPalindromeRecursive(left, right) {
    // Base case: when there are no characters left to compare
    if (left >= right) {
      return true;
    }

    // Ignore non-alphanumeric characters
    if (!isAlphanumeric(str.charAt(left))) {
      return isPalindromeRecursive(left + 1, right);
    }
    if (!isAlphanumeric(str.charAt(right))) {
      return isPalindromeRecursive(left, right - 1);
    }

    // Ignore case sensitivity by converting both characters to lowercase
    var leftChar = str.charAt(left).toLowerCase();
    var rightChar = str.charAt(right).toLowerCase();

    // If the characters are not equal, it is not a palindrome
    if (leftChar !== rightChar) {
      return false;
    }

    // Recursive call to check the next pair of characters
    return isPalindromeRecursive(left + 1, right - 1);
  }

  // Start the recursive function with the entire string
  return isPalindromeRecursive(0, str.length - 1);
}

// Example usage:
console.log(isPalindrome("A man, a plan, a canal, Panama")); // true
console.log(isPalindrome("race car")); // true
console.log(isPalindrome("hello world")); // false
```

In this implementation, we have two helper functions: `isAlphanumeric()` and `isPalindromeRecursive()`. The `isAlphanumeric()` function checks whether a character is alphanumeric using its Unicode value. The `isPalindromeRecursive()` function is the main recursive function that checks if the remaining characters form a palindrome by comparing the characters from the left and right ends of the string.

We start the recursion with the `isPalindromeRecursive()` function, passing the starting and ending indices of the string. The function first checks if there are no characters left to compare, in which case it returns `true`. Then, it checks if the characters at the left and right indices are non-alphanumeric and skips them by calling itself recursively with updated indices. Next, it converts the characters to lowercase to ignore case sensitivity and compares them. If they are not equal, it returns `false`. If they are equal, it calls itself recursively with updated indices to check the next pair of characters.

