Here is a JavaScript program that compares two strings for equality, ignoring case, without using any built-in methods:

```javascript
function compareStringsIgnoreCase(string1, string2) {
  // Check if the lengths of the two strings are different
  if (string1.length !== string2.length) {
    return false;
  }

  // Iterate through each character in the strings
  for (let i = 0; i < string1.length; i++) {
    // Get the ASCII codes of the characters
    const charCode1 = string1.charCodeAt(i);
    const charCode2 = string2.charCodeAt(i);

    // Check if the characters are different
    if (charCode1 !== charCode2) {
      // Check if the characters are letters
      if (isLetter(charCode1) && isLetter(charCode2)) {
        // Convert the characters to lowercase and compare again
        if (toLowerCase(charCode1) !== toLowerCase(charCode2)) {
          return false;
        }
      } else {
        return false;
      }
    }
  }

  return true;
}

// Helper function to check if a character is a letter
function isLetter(charCode) {
  return (charCode >= 65 && charCode <= 90) || (charCode >= 97 && charCode <= 122);
}

// Helper function to convert a character to lowercase
function toLowerCase(charCode) {
  return charCode >= 65 && charCode <= 90 ? charCode + 32 : charCode;
}

// Test the program
const string1 = 'Hello';
const string2 = 'heLLo';

console.log(compareStringsIgnoreCase(string1, string2)); // Output: true
```

In this program, we define the `compareStringsIgnoreCase` function that takes two strings as input. 

First, we check if the lengths of the two strings are different. If they are, we immediately return false because the strings cannot be equal.

Next, we iterate through each character in the strings using a for loop. For each character, we get the ASCII code using the `charCodeAt` method.

If the ASCII codes of the characters are different, we check if both characters are letters using the `isLetter` helper function. If they are, we convert the characters to lowercase using the `toLowerCase` helper function and compare them again. If the lowercase characters are still different, we return false.

If the ASCII codes of the characters are the same or they are different letters that become the same when converted to lowercase, we continue to the next character.

After iterating through all the characters, if we haven't returned false, it means that the strings are equal, ignoring case, so we return true.

The `isLetter` helper function checks if a character code corresponds to an uppercase or lowercase letter.

The `toLowerCase` helper function converts an uppercase letter to lowercase by adding 32 to its ASCII code. It doesn't change the ASCII code if the character is already lowercase.

Finally, we test the program by comparing the strings 'Hello' and 'heLLo'. The program should return true because the strings are equal, ignoring case.

